00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
we got our hands on the call of duty  vanguard beta build a little early so  i'm here to tell you what's new and if  it's worth checking out when the beta  goes public later this month our goal  here is to have a little fun talk about  the new call of duty and genuinely  figure out which audience vanguard is  for by breaking down what we were able  to see  of course our experiences with this  closed beta may differ from what the  public beta will offer maps modes and  weapons are still being tweaked so just  keep that in mind as we continue if you  enjoy this breakdown stay tuned by  subscribing to gamespot for continued  coverage of call of duty battlefield  halo and more let's get into it  as we were waiting for the lobbies to  build up we were able to see newly added  game modes weapons perks attachments and  way more let's begin with the gunsmith  it's back and it's nearly identical to  the one featured in warzone and modern  warfare 2019  like before as you level up your weapon  categories for unlockables begin to  build up such as scopes stocks and  barrels i was able to peek at an stg44  blueprint and confirmed that all assault  rifles can have up to 10 attachments the  blueprint that they featured didn't even  have them all filled out while we're on  the topic the attachments in general  were plentiful that's probably because  you can now rank your weapons up to  level 70.  my jaw dropped looking at the amount of  scopes alone the variety here is  honestly commendable but it's similar to  modern warfare 2019 where you can  actually build a bad gun if you wanted  to and i really dig that but remember in  call of duty world at war or even world  war ii where there were just a few  fictional scopes that were sort of  tacked on in vanguard that dial is  turned up to 11. it looks like for the  sake of fun gunplay reality is taking a  backseat and i think this will prevent  these classic world war ii weapons from  feeling stale all of this is also  probably in preparation for the eventual  merge with war zone you can even unlock  alternate iron sights which is cool or  replace your iron sights with this film  projector  just kidding it's an infrared scope what  the beta also had nearly every weapon  unlocked if you've ever played a world  war ii game you'll be familiar with the  offerings the mp40 thompson the bar and  the combat knife using these longer  range rifles like ars snipers and lmgs  on these bigger maps felt like  stretching their legs it felt good to  use them at long ranges compared to the  smaller maps on champion hill  speaking of which the maps we did play  on were pretty different from each other  and they played well in most game modes  we tried there was a pretty big snowy  city map that took place in a town  square  a really green beach side map a hotel  map with roof access that was pretty  cool and there was a mountaintop map  that was called eagle's nest that mostly  took place indoors these maps featured  more destructible cover just like the  alpha and in my experience i found it to  be helpful in some situations to peak or  take a second to reload instead of  running for cover every time i needed to  some form of broken cover made it a  little easier to hide for a second and  then jump right back into a gunfight  which kept the pacing pretty brisk i  wouldn't say these maps are porous like  modern warfare 2019's where there's a  lot of places to hide and camp however  what i enjoyed and what kept it separate  from modern warfare 2019 was that you  were able to break these small forms of  cover entirely if they were being  overused  so in my experience it didn't really get  campy especially in blitz mode  speaking of blitz mode we tested out the  combat pacing for certain game modes  there were three  tactical which hosts smaller 6v6 matches  assault which is anywhere from 20 to 28  players and blitz mode which can host up  to 48 players and 24 v24  blitz mode was actually pretty crazy i  wasn't expecting the combat pacings to  be so different from each other but  blitz mode actually was  it felt like ground war except on maps  that weren't designed for ground war it  was definitely a highlight for me we  played other modes like team deathmatch  domination kill confirmed like i  mentioned and patrol which is their  hardpoint mode where the hardpoint is  continually moving and that mode was  pretty fun i wouldn't say any of these  game modes were new and exciting but  classic a good way to just test  everything out all of these maps and  modes that we played have been confirmed  by sledgehammer to be in the open beta  as well as the return of champion hill  perks kill streaks and field upgrades  are back and most of them are familiar  if you've played a call of duty game in  the last few years  i am sad to report that dogs are back  please stop making me kill dogs in games  some issues with the alpha build have  been addressed with the beta when you're  wounded and on the brink of death your  screen doesn't dim so harshly as it did  before  lighting has also been tweaked and in my  opinion it was easier to see enemies  more issues did pop up through our  playthrough but nothing game breaking  there are small graphical glitches like  this shoulder one some server issues and  most agreed that the volume overall was  kind of low also in our play test on  console there was no sign of the fov  slider  on a more personal note i didn't have  much luck with the shotgun class  up close in a firefight i lost to an  stg44  at point blank  i know this person had shots on me and  killed me first but i think the shot i  landed should have killed them too what  do you think  [Music]  another gripe for me might be the  goliath which is a remote controlled  kill streak it's pretty funny to use  this thing and like run people over with  it and detonate it but it has trouble  driving over terrain and gets caught on  a lot of stuff even dead bodies which  was pretty funny  we got eyes there was also that beach  map that i mentioned which looks really  nice but it's easy to get pinned down in  your spawn especially when you're  playing something like domination  specifically blitz domination  oh wow i guess he felt the same way but  aside from these smaller issues we  encountered i can personally say i had  fun and when i reflect on this beta i  think of the maps blitz mode and how the  guns we've seen for years actually felt  kind of different and fun  the beta build gave me the real  multiplayer test i wanted and while all  of this may change after the call of  duty honeymoon phase in a few months i  can safely say that i enjoyed my time  with the build as is right now despite  being yet another world war ii shooter  vanguard's multiplayer felt like it had  fans of newer call of duty games in mind  especially in terms of the user  interface and weapons perks and field  upgrades all of that just reminds me of  modern warfare 2019 but at least in my  playthrough it feels like they left some  of those problems behind such as super  porous map design which leads to slower  paced gun fights in vanguard things felt  more hectic and gunfights were  constantly happening especially in the  blitz mode as i mentioned  if you're excited to try out this beta  build let me know in the comments but  also let me know if this isn't the kind  of call of duty you're interested in  we've got way more vanguard coverage  coming up so stay tuned to gamespot for  more videos like this coming soon  okay i'll then on some gameplay here  that i thought was pretty funny we were  playing patrol which is that moving  hardpoint mode and we were doing well  until who is immortal dropped a glide  bomb on our entire team take a look  [Music]  hope you enjoyed that i couldn't help  but laugh i'll see you next time  you

